5
00:00:16,340 --> 00:00:17,860
one patch of membrane.

6
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:22,660
The remaining question is how that event travels, and

7
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:26,580
why it does not bounce backwards, when sodium ions

8
00:00:26,580 --> 00:00:30,440
enter during depolarization, the inside

9
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:33,100
of that region becomes positive.

10
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:37,140
Those positive charges spread sideways as local

11
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:37,620
currents.

12
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:43,220
Just ahead of the active region, the local current depolarizes

13
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:45,920
the neighboring membrane towards threshold.

14
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:50,420
Voltage -gated sodium channels are open, and a new

15
00:00:50,420 --> 00:00:51,980
action potential is born.

16
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:56,520
The impulse is not a single particle racing down the axon.

17
00:00:57,700 --> 00:01:01,500
Is a chain of action potentials each triggering the

18
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:01,700
nerve?

19
00:01:02,460 --> 00:01:06,080
Why does the impulse not also travel backwards into membrane

20
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:07,940
that is just fired?

21
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:11,320
The answer is the refractory period.

22
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:15,820
Immediately after an action potential, voltage -gated sodium

23
00:01:15,820 --> 00:01:17,920
channels are inactivated.

24
00:01:18,410 --> 00:01:22,040
For a short time, no new action potential can start

25
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:24,840
in that patch, however strong the stimulus.

26
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:28,720
That is the absolute refractory period.

27
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:33,160
As the sodium channels recover from inactivation, a

28
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:36,780
stronger than normal stimulus can start another action potential.

29
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:41,360
That interval is the relative refractory period, and

30
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:43,440
it overlaps with hyperpolarization.

31
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:47,840
When the membrane is even further from threshold, together

32
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:51,700
these refractory stages do two important jobs.

33
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:56,440
They force one -way propagation towards resting membrane

34
00:01:56,440 --> 00:02:00,220
ahead, and away from inactivated membrane behind.

35
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:04,640
They also set a maximum firing frequency, because the second

36
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:07,800
spike cannot start until the sodium channels have recovered.

37
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:12,100
In an un -milded axon, every neighboring patch

38
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:13,920
must depolarize in turn.

39
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:16,320
The impulse creeps along the membrane.

40
00:02:16,980 --> 00:02:20,160
In a malinated axon, the picture changes.

41
00:02:21,620 --> 00:02:23,300
My lean is an insulator.

42
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:27,860
Ion flow across the membrane is largely restricted to the nodes of

43
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:31,060
Ranveer, where voltage -gated channels are concentrated.

44
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:35,140
Local currents travel quickly under the myelane to the next node.

45
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:39,220
The action potential appears to jump from node

46
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:42,640
to node, that is, solitory conduction.

47
00:02:43,380 --> 00:02:47,480
From the Latin for leaping, solitory conduction is

48
00:02:47,480 --> 00:02:50,060
faster and more energy efficient.

49
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:54,920
Fewer ions need to be pumped back, because fewer stretches

50
00:02:54,920 --> 00:02:56,840
of membrane exchange ions.

51
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:01,220
Increasing axon diameter also speeds conduction,

52
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:05,760
because local currents travel more easily inside a wider axon.

53
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:10,560
Those two adaptations, myelin and diameter, explain

54
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:14,080
why some fibers, carry urgent signals so quickly.

55
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:17,300
